---
description: Ask before string lists, regex, or substring membership
alwaysApply: true
---

# No hand-built string vocabularies

Do not add a list of string literals, a regex, or `"str" in "str2"` /
`needle in haystack` to decide what text means or whether two names
match. Those fail silent when the vocabulary is incomplete (Horshat /
Horashat / Hurshat; Achziv with no Hebrew alias).

**Last resort only, and ask first.** If a list, regex, or substring
check still looks necessary, say so in the conversation and wait for a
yes. Do not add it quietly.

```python
# BAD — aliases in code; ILIKE / `in` as "close enough"
_NAMED_CAMPSITE_ALIASES = {"horshat tal": "חורשת טל", ...}
if "achziv" in name.casefold():
    ...

# GOOD — campsites.english_name + pg_trgm (similarity / word_similarity).
# scrape-sites fills English with one 235B call over every Hebrew name.
# Typos (Horashat / Horshat) and a short name inside a long title are
# ranked in SQL; there is no alias list.
```

Still fine without asking:

- Equality of two values you already have (`a == b`, `key in dict`)
- A closed enumeration the code itself defines (`SubjectCategory`,
  `QualifierUnit`)
- Edit distance / pg_trgm between two strings

`naming.opposed()` / `ANTONYM_PAIRS` is the existing sanctioned
exception (measured 2026-09-04; see `llm-decides-semantics.mdc`).
